    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d316">

                <pb facs="0747" n="699" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v06p1/pg699"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">741.13/11–1051</note>
                <head>No. 316<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">President Truman to Prime Minister
                        Churchill</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d316-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1951-11-10">November 10, 1951</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The January 3 date for our meeting is entirely satisfactory, and I look forward
                    to seeing you then. I shall also be happy to see Mr. Eden <pb facs="0748"
                        n="700" xml:id="pg700"/> again, and will welcome Lord Ismay and your
                    military advisors.</p>
                <p>I appreciate your good wishes for Florida sunshine during my stay at Key
                    West.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d316-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d316-f1-content">
                <p>The source text was attached to a telegram from the Key West White House asking
                    that it be sent to Prime Minister Churchill. A handwritten note on the source
                    text indicates that it was handed to Ambassador Franks’ secretary on Nov.
                    11.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
